WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.629
Fictional Cedar Service is replacing email handoffs with a shared ticket queue.

00:00:04.869 --> 00:00:13.680
The desired behavior is specific: before ending a shift, each coordinator assigns every open ticket to an owner and records the next action.

00:00:13.920 --> 00:00:16.821
Installing the tool does not establish that behavior.

00:00:17.511 --> 00:00:20.754
Prosci’s ADKAR model concerns individual change.

00:00:20.994 --> 00:00:25.666
Its five elements are Awareness, Desire, Knowledge, Ability and Reinforcement.

00:00:25.906 --> 00:00:31.730
In brief: understand the reason; choose to engage; learn what to do; demonstrate it; keep it going.

00:00:31.970 --> 00:00:35.511
The model is one part of a wider change-management approach.

00:00:36.201 --> 00:00:40.660
This sample uses original conversations to explore those distinctions.

00:00:40.900 --> 00:00:43.481
It is not an official diagnostic instrument.

00:00:43.721 --> 00:00:49.183
Treat a possible barrier as a hypothesis to discuss, rather than a score that defines someone.

00:00:49.873 --> 00:00:56.550
Worked example: a coordinator can explain the new handoff steps but has never completed them in the live workflow.

00:00:56.790 --> 00:00:58.539
You have some evidence of knowledge.

00:00:58.779 --> 00:01:04.155
You do not yet have evidence that the person can carry out the behavior in realistic conditions.

00:01:04.395 --> 00:01:07.894
Record exactly what you observed before choosing a response.

00:01:08.584 --> 00:01:09.672
Now it's your turn.

00:01:09.912 --> 00:01:13.368
Which success statement is most observable for Cedar Service?

00:01:13.608 --> 00:01:16.723
